reviewer_objections.rebuttal:
We acknowledge both explicitly. The contributions are:
(a) a closed-form identifiability bound for camera-to-camera offsets from dense fusion residuals in a dynamic scene, with static/dynamic decoupling of pose and time, validated empirically;
(b) the coupling in which the observability source (the velocity field) also drives uncertainty-aware compensation;
(c) an exact-GT benchmark.

The experiments include the direct VINS-style sparse-feature transplant and VisualSync (median error under 50 ms, offline) as baselines, and we show the dense 3D residual reaches sub-2 ms accuracy online.

novelty.remaining_novel_angle:
What remains defensible is a real-capture benchmark for online multi-view RGB-D telepresence synthesis, scored at non-rig viewpoints. Its ground truth would come from genlocked witness cameras that replay recorded HMD head trajectories, including extrapolation off the rig hull, plus a real 63 mm binocular witness pair. I found no 2023-2026 dataset, in vision, graphics, VR or multimedia, that gives real captured moving plus binocular ground truth for a fixed calibrated RGB-D capture rig of humans. The nearest cases are MV2 (driving, moving ground truth, ECCV 2026), DyCheck (monocular input, static test cameras) and StereoGenBench/Syn4D (synthetic).

A second defensible piece is a view-synthesis-specific form of streaming accuracy. When a deadline is missed, the output is stale in two ways: the scene time is old, and the viewer pose is old because the head has moved. Late depth-based reprojection is allowed and scored, so methods earn credit for geometry that supports timewarp.

The empirical headline, that method rankings on rig-interpolation benchmarks flip under moving-viewer, binocular and deadline scoring, is untested and would carry the paper.

These parts are not new and must be credited:
- the deadline scoring rule itself (Li et al., ECCV 2020 streaming accuracy, and its 3D-detection and event-perception successors);
- 'minute-long multi-view takes' (PackUV-2B, ImViD);
- 'multi-view sensor-depth human capture' (ViVo, HUM4D-style RGB-D);
- desync perturbation splits (Sync-NeRF desynchronized N3DV);
- moving-camera pose registration inside a static studio (Movi-Cam-style checkerboard tracking);
- a 2026 streaming-FVV benchmark with a latency track (M3ISR, synthetic, uncoupled).

A cheaper spoiler exists. A synthetic telepresence benchmark (Syn4D- or StereoGenBench-style) could supply moving and binocular ground truth for free. The paper must therefore argue that real sensor-depth noise, genlock or desync and non-Lambertian human content change the rankings.

reviewer_objections.rebuttal:
VSCP is static, per scene, split-conformal and non-generative. Ours contributes four things VSCP lacks:
(i) a live calibration feed that exists only on a synchronised rig;
(ii) the dropout ladder for extrapolated views;
(iii) stratification by geometric provenance, which the ablation shows gives at least 2x tighter bounds on observed pixels than difficulty-only stratification (a VSCP-style baseline is in the table);
(iv) certified hallucination-rate control for a generative component, which is the question telepresence actually asks.

novelty.remaining_novel_angle:
I found no 2024-2026 work that combines the following into one ONLINE, FEED-FORWARD, fixed-rig multi-view VIDEO pipeline:
(i) explicitly estimate a per-frame second 'reflection depth' by plane-sweeping only the cross-view-inconsistent (specular) residual at TSDF surface points;
(ii) forward-splat diffuse and specular layers with separate depths;
(iii) give them different temporal filters: heavy TSDF memory for diffuse, low-inertia reflection-depth-warped memory for specular;
(iv) evaluate with specular-region-restricted temporal and view-consistency metrics (EPI/STED/TCC on highlight masks) on telepresence content (glasses, skin, screens).

The two-layer representation itself is NOT novel (Sinha 2012; Xu 2021, which the ideator did not cite). Implicit multi-layer handling of reflections from fixed-rig video is also NOT novel (DeepView/Broxton 2020; Quark 2024 for real time). Cross-view photometric dispersion as a specular detector is textbook MVS.

The defensible claim is therefore narrow: the first online, temporally filtered two-layer reflection IBR, with layer-specific temporal dynamics that resolve the lag-versus-flicker trade-off, plus a specular telepresence consistency benchmark.

Idea B is weaker. Probabilistic per-view depth distributions fused along target rays and trained end-to-end are established: Soft3D 2017, Self-Supervised Visibility Learning CVPR 2021, NeuRay CVPR 2022. Per-pixel Kalman depth filtering and stereo uncertainty heads are also classic. What remains is the systems finding: calibrated stereo-foundation-model uncertainty (mono-stereo disagreement), carried through a real-time fusion that is temporal and Kalman-filtered, gating a geometry-warp versus Unstructured-Lumigraph-style fallback. Its most publishable piece is probably the diagnostic that explains the base paper's null TSDF ablation, not the fusion operator.

novelty.best_variant:
Idea 1 (two-layer, reflection-aware, geometry-guided IBR) has the more defensible novelty. Its core novelty lies in things no found work does: an explicit online reflection-depth layer in a feed-forward multi-view video pipeline, specular-aware (1 − s) TSDF weighting, and layer-specific temporal filtering. The closest threats (Xu 2021, Broxton 2020, Quark 2024) are respectively offline and static, implicit rather than reflection-specific, or silent on reflections and temporal stability.

To survive review it must:
- cite and compare against Xu 2021 and a Quark-style layered baseline;
- present the temporal-dynamics split (diffuse = high inertia, specular = low inertia, warped by reflection depth) as the headline, not the two-layer representation itself;
- ship the specular-masked view × time consistency benchmark.

A strong combination is to fold Idea 2's cheap uncertainty signal (mono-stereo disagreement plus cross-view dispersion) into Idea 1 as the specular/unreliable-geometry detector and the gate for the glossy (non-mirror) fallback. The 'light-field fallback' then becomes the answer for glossy cases where reflection depth is meaningless, instead of a standalone contribution that the Soft3D/NeuRay/SSVL lineage already covers.

review.first_two_week_test:
A training-free headroom-and-feasibility probe on DyNeRF (the cook_spinach and flame_steak scenes, or others with hand motion) and on 3-5 DNA-Rendering sequences with fast arm and hand motion. Use the base pipeline if the code is available; otherwise use a forward point-splat plus TSDF re-implementation, or GPS-Gaussian as a stand-in renderer.

For Δ ∈ {33, 67, 100, 167, 233} ms, render at held-out cameras:
- (1) stale: frame t;
- (2) stale reprojection;
- (3) 2D constant-velocity flow warp of the stale render;
- (4) 3D constant-velocity advection of per-view depth points, using depth from t-1 and t plus RAFT flow lifted to 3D, then re-splatted;
- (5) oracle: true inputs at t+Δ.

Compute motion-masked LPIPS and RTMPose hand/wrist keypoint error against ground truth at t+Δ. Report the headroom (oracle versus stale) and the fraction recovered by (3) and (4).

In parallel, spend 2-3 days measuring latency asymmetry. Time the stages of a simple capture→depth→fuse→encode→network→render loop against a 20 ms Opus-style audio path, which gives a realistic Δ_a. On 20 Multiface speech clips, compute LSE-D for a stale mouth versus an off-the-shelf 2D lip-sync editor driven by audio at the display time, applied per view, at a 100 ms lead.

Go signal:
- the headroom at Δ = 100 ms is clearly visible, with motion-masked LPIPS gap ≥ 0.03 and wrist error ≥ 8 px at 640 px width;
- 3D constant-velocity advection recovers ≥ 25% of it without learning;
- 3D advection beats 2D warping at held-out views;
- the measured Δ_a is ≥ 50 ms.

reviewer_objections.rebuttal:
The paper concedes this explicitly and does not claim 3D-over-2D as its contribution. The new parts are four:
- a causal, feed-forward, cross-subject forecaster that runs at ≤ 10 ms, whereas the per-scene offline methods take minutes to hours per scene and are included as baselines on causal windows;
- a time-aligned, latency-charged evaluation protocol that changes method rankings;
- a no-regret hedge whose worst-case regret is measured;
- the audio-lead observation and mouth forecasting.
None of these appears in the prior work.

review.first_two_week_test:
Goal: test C1 on public data without the learned blend network and without a full reimplementation of Ha et al.

Week 1:
- Download about 30 RH20T sequences from one robot configuration, all involving grasping or placing.
- Build a geometry-only renderer:
  - forward-splat each view's RGB-D into the held-out fixed camera;
  - build the per-target image-space TSDF from K = 4 nearest fixed views;
  - render depth and apply a simple visibility-weighted colour blend.
- Rasterise the robot mesh from joint angles (pyrender or nvdiffrast), using the provided calibration.
- Check alignment of the mesh silhouette against the images. The IoU of the mesh mask against a SAM2 gripper mask gives an FK/calibration error estimate.

Week 2: compare five variants on EE-region depth AbsRel against held-out depth, flying-pixel rate, and EE-crop LPIPS:
- (a) fixed cameras only;
- (b) (a) plus the wrist camera at FK pose;
- (c) (b) plus the photometric pose refinement;
- (d) (c) plus the robot mesh as a TSDF input;
- (e) (c) plus segment-and-composite of the URDF render.

Also run a 10-minute informal pilot: 3 lab members watch (a) versus (d) videos in the HMD and judge the gripper-object gap.

Cost: about 1 GPU and 1 student. The validation signal is (d) beating (a) by at least 25% on EE depth error and at least 40% on flying pixels, and beating (e) on occlusion-boundary F-score.

novelty.remaining_novel_angle:
The static first-order core is not new. Displacement ~ f·b·ε_z/z² times the texture gradient, its baseline dependence, and depth-uncertainty-derived blending weights are established in the 3D-video coding literature (Kim & Ortega 2009; Fang et al. TIP 2014; TIP 2016; D-NOSE 2011; Bi & Liang 2025), in Pujades et al. CVPR 2014, and in Lin & Shum IJCV 2004. The DIBR quality-assessment literature (Liu et al. TIP 2015; ICIP 2016) already separates flicker from spatial distortion empirically.

I found nothing, 2024-2026 included, on:
(a) a temporal bias/variance decomposition of multi-view depth error, with autocorrelation and K-set view switching, that separately predicts PSNR (bias) and flicker metrics (STED, TCC; variance);
(b) an analytic account of temporal filters and TSDF fusion in online K-view IBR with learned blend weights (they reduce variance, not bias);
(c) a formal crossover between geometry-guided warp-and-blend and learned or geometry-free renderers (LVSM-class, ENeRF, GPS-Gaussian), modelled as conditional-mean estimators and validated by controlled depth-noise sweeps on synthetic dynamic scenes;
(d) a theory-derived per-pixel Geometry Advantage Index and a rig and sensor design guide aimed at flicker targets.

The paper must be framed as extending the classic DIBR synthesis-distortion models to temporal statistics and learned renderers, citing Fang 2014, Kim-Ortega 2009 and Pujades 2014, rather than claiming the propagation model itself. Two caveats. The motivating 'puzzle' rests on one unreplicated DyNeRF ablation in which only STED moves. The GAI hybrid router on its own is close to existing confidence-gated hybrids. Coverage caveat: WebSearch was exhausted and the Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex and arXiv APIs were rate-limited, so a concurrent analysis paper in the immersive-video (MPEG MIV) or coding community may have been missed.
